<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<kml v="1" site="caverns.custom" epoch="1">
  <head>
    <title>Crystal Caverns</title>
    <meta name="description" content="A first-person 3D maze game, drawn in software by a 15 KB WebAssembly module inside the Kickl sandbox."/>
    <meta name="headline" content="A first-person 3D game in 15 KB, running in a sandbox with no network, no files and no clock."/>
    <meta name="author" content="Kickl"/>
    <meta name="date" content="2026-10-27"/>
    <meta name="tags" content="games, wasm, 3d, raycaster, demo"/>
  </head>
  <body>
    <page>
      <h1>Crystal Caverns</h1>
      <badge>webassembly · 3d · no imports · 15 KB</badge>

      <p>Find the eight crystals hidden in the cave, then walk into the exit portal that opens. Every cave is different: it
      grows from a seed the browser picks. Your best time is saved, encrypted, in your own profile.</p>

      <game id="caverns" label="Crystal Caverns"/>

      <p><b>Controls.</b> Left and Right turn, Up and Down walk, A and B sidestep, Start begins. With a mouse, hold the button down
      and the game steers towards the pointer and walks forward; hold it low on the screen to walk backwards.</p>

      <h2>What you are looking at</h2>
      <p>Nothing here is a video, an image or a library. The module is a few kilobytes of arithmetic that fills a 240 by 160 picture
      thirty times a second, and the browser hands it nothing else: no network, no files, no clock, no randomness except the seed.</p>
      <p>Each frame the game does all of this itself:</p>
      <quote>casts one ray per screen column through a maze to find the wall it hits · textures that wall and shades it by torchlight
      and by the coloured glow of nearby crystals · draws a perspective floor and ceiling · sorts and draws glowing crystals and an
      exit portal in front of or behind the walls · throws bursts of 3D particles · and draws a map and a pixel font.</quote>
      <p>Even the stone, moss and glowing runes are computed from noise when the game starts, which is why the whole thing fits in
      15 KB. It uses roughly half of the instruction budget the browser allows a game on an unclaimed name.</p>

      <h2>Make one yourself</h2>
      <p>This game is written in AssemblyScript, a TypeScript-like language that compiles to WebAssembly. The tutorial in
      docs/26-game-tutorial.md starts with a gradient and a bouncing square and builds up, step by step, to this cave, and shows
      how to measure that your own game fits the budget before you publish it.</p>
    </page>
  </body>
</kml>
